# trace of one row of each kind shows no mutate_*.sh in any of them.  So the
# five harnesses TRACK MUTAUDIT fixed, the 21 rows TRACK REANCHOR re-anchored
# and the 9 TRACK CBANCHOR restored were all protecting evidence that only ran
# when a human typed the script's name.
#
# THESE THREE ROWS ARE NOT AN ATTEMPT TO SCHEDULE THE OTHER SIXTY.  Running the
# mutants is minutes to hours and is shared cost paid by every track on every
# run; what actually destroyed evidence three times is an anchor going dead,
# which is a TEXT property and is nearly free to check.  sim:mutaudit is that
# check for all 63 at once.  The two harness rows are the only two in the whole
# set that satisfy all of: a verdict-derived exit code, under 60 s, under
# 100 MB, and RTL that ships.  Everything else is recorded as MANUAL in
# sim/mutation_harness_wiring.tsv with the reason and the measured cost.
#
#   sim:mutaudit  sim/check_mutation_harness.py -- every sim/mutate_*.sh is in
#       sim/mutation_harness_audit.tsv and none has the zero-match-anchor
#       defect (a mutation that did not apply reported as SURVIVED).
#       MEASURED 2026-09-20: 0.02 s, 11,712 kB peak RSS.
#       READ THE PRECONDITION IN THE PATCH HEADER BEFORE ENABLING THIS ROW.
#   sim:mutrms    sim/mutate_rmsnorm_rs_mem.sh -- 10 mutants of the shipping
#       banked RMSNorm store over three check configurations (FULL, noVAL,
#       noVL+LT), 7 of them pinned to an expected verdict; the script exits
#       nonzero when any verdict differs from the one written down for it, or
#       when a sed matched nothing.  MEASURED 2026-09-20: 56.3 s, 18,272 kB.
#   sim:mutbfm    sim/mutate_rmsnorm_bf_mem.sh -- the same shape over the bf
#       store, 14 mutants of which 10 are pinned.  MEASURED: 46.0 s, 18,192 kB.
#
# WHY A SCRATCH OVERRIDE IS PASSED.  Both harnesses default their scratch to a
# FIXED absolute path (/mnt/storage/rmsmux/mut, /mnt/storage/bfmem/mut), so two
# gates running at once would collide there.  run_selfcheck runs its command
# unquoted and NOT through a shell, so a `VAR=val cmd` prefix would be handed to
# argv; `env` is a real binary and is the form that works.  $SCRATCH is the
# gate's own scratch root and is in scope here (set at line 1460).
printf 'mutaudit\tsim\tRUN\t-\t-\t-\t-\n' >> "$PLAN"
